22:27:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question, and it touches on one of the most fascinating differences between herbivores like horses and omnivores like humans. The short answer is that **horses don’t actually build all that muscle on straw alone**, and their incredible digestive system allows them to turn fibrous, low-protein plant material into high-quality protein—something we simply can’t do. Let’s unpack it. --- ### 1. The “straw” misconception First, horses don’t normally eat just straw. Straw is the dried stalks of cereal plants and is very low in protein (around 3–4%) and energy. A horse on a straw-only diet would lose muscle and condition. When people picture a muscular horse eating “straw,” they usually mean **hay** (dried grass or legumes like alfalfa). Good-quality grass hay typically contains **8–14% crude protein**, which is enough for maintenance and moderate work. Even that might seem low compared to a human bodybuilder’s diet, but this is where the horse’s digestive physiology takes over. --- ### 2. Horses are hindgut fermentation factories Horses are **non-ruminant herbivores**, meaning they ferment plant fiber in a huge, specialized part of the large intestine—the **cecum and colon** (hindgut). This is packed with billions of microbes: bacteria, protozoa, and fungi. These microbes produce enzymes that break down cellulose and other complex carbohydrates that humans can’t digest at all. The breakdown products are volatile fatty acids, which the horse absorbs and uses as its main energy source. But here’s the key to your question: **those same microbes don’t just produce energy—they also manufacture protein.** --- ### 3. Microbial protein synthesis The microorganisms in the horse’s hindgut can take **non‑protein nitrogen** (like urea, which the horse naturally recycles from its bloodstream into the gut) and even the simple nitrogen compounds in plant material, and build them into their own bodies as **high-quality microbial pr